{"name":"Blue Torus","hex":"#042993","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"torus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The standard representation of such a space in 3-dimensional Euclidean space: a surface or solid formed by rotating a closed curve, especially a circle, about a line which lies in the same plane but does not intersect it (e.g. like a ring doughnut).","A topological space which is a product of two circles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/torus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Torus","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a torus is a surface of revolution generated by revolving a circle in three-dimensional space one full revolution about an axis that is coplanar with the circle. The main types of tori include ring tori, horn tori, and spindle tori. A ring torus is sometimes colloquially referred to as a doughnut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Tesseract_torus.png/330px-Tesseract_torus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torus","wikidata":"Q12510"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Ring_Torus_to_Degenerate_Torus_%28Short%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Ring_Torus_to_Degenerate_Torus_%28Short%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"As the distance from the axis of revolution decreases, the ring torus becomes a horn torus, then a spindle torus, and finally degenerates into a double-covered sphere.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARing_Torus_to_Degenerate_Torus_(Short).gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Standard_torus-ring.png/500px-Standard_torus-ring.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Standard_torus-ring.png/1280px-Standard_torus-ring.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Standard_torus-ring.png/500px-Standard_torus-ring.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStandard_torus-ring.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Standard_torus-horn.png/500px-Standard_torus-horn.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, http